Derive a wall pressure from momentum transfer and velocity moments (@physics-molecular-pressure: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/properties/Equations-of-state-and-caloric-information.lingua#r_3YYAN5FQJXPX2441B2ZK5M4VPN") { r_0E97E7965A096183F866D6F07C
Consider a deliberately simplified classical model: identical point particles of mass m in a stationary cube of side ℓ, with negligible forces between collisions and perfectly elastic reflections from its walls. Ignore gravity and particle interactions for the following trajectory calculation. A particle with nonzero velocity component v_x reaches the same x-normal wall once every 2ℓ/|v_x| in steady repeated motion. Each collision transfers normal momentum magnitude 2m|v_x| to that wall. Their quotient gives time-averaged force m v_x²/ℓ. If v_x=0, its contribution is zero; do not divide by a zero component.

Add particle contributions and divide by wall area ℓ². With V=ℓ³, p_x=(m/V)Σv_ix². Analogous expressions hold for y and z. These are wall/time averages under the stated model, not the instantaneous force during a collision. Reuse [Relate forces to momentum and system boundaries](Relate-forces-to-momentum-and-system-boundaries.lingua#r_07F88E984BB76BB449F22A9467) for impulse and [Use linear combinations to explain span independence and coordinates](../../mathematics/core/Use-linear-combinations-to-explain-span-independence-and-coordinates.lingua#r_004D242DF23E1A3D1718672EAE) for component accounts. Taking longer averages can suppress some fluctuations without proving the assumptions about collisions, stationarity or the gas are correct.

Write ⟨v_x²⟩=(1/N)Σv_ix² for an equal-particle average. A classical equilibrium gas with no preferred direction has equal directional second moments, giving pV=(Nm/3)⟨v²⟩=(2/3)K_trans, where K_trans=(m/2)Σv_i² is translational kinetic energy in the rest frame of the macroscopic gas. Compare this with the equilibrium ideal-gas equation pV=Nk_B T=nRT: K_trans=(3/2)Nk_B T. The pressure/momentum calculation and the equilibrium equation together supply this interpretation. They do not derive equilibrium or a full velocity distribution from one measured pressure.

Average velocity and average squared speed are different. For a finite set of equal-mass particles, let u=⟨v⟩ and c_i=v_i−u. Since Σc_i=0, expanding squares gives Σ|v_i|²=N|u|²+Σ|c_i|². The first term is organized mean motion; the second is motion relative to it. Pressure in a flowing local-equilibrium description involves the relative velocities, with further continuum assumptions. Do not insert a bulk flow speed into the random-motion temperature formula. Reuse [Describe a chance quantity by its weighted mean and spread](../../inquiry/core/Describe-a-chance-quantity-by-its-weighted-mean-and-spread.lingua#r_0A9487B256CD8C0ED0083290DA) for the algebra of means and spread while distinguishing a finite sample average from a stipulated probability expectation.

An original arithmetic fixture uses eight particles with the eight velocity triples (±2,±2,±2) in units v₀, mass m₀ and cube side 2ℓ₀. The mean velocity is zero, directional pressures are (4,4,4) in units p₀=m₀v₀²/ℓ₀³, and K_trans=48m₀v₀². Their pressure-volume relation checks the formula. Replacing the triples by (±4,±1,±1) gives pressures (16,1,1)p₀ and energy 72m₀v₀². Zero mean velocity still holds, but a single direction-independent pressure does not. Even the first eight-direction table has only selected symmetric moments, not a fully rotationally invariant equilibrium distribution. No thermodynamic temperature or equilibration claim is assigned to either finite fixture.

For a monatomic ideal gas in a classical regime where internal excitation and interparticle potential energy can be neglected, take U=(3/2)nRT plus a reference constant. Rotational, vibrational, electronic and interaction contributions can change the caloric model in other regimes. Equal p,V,n,T information alone cannot determine those additional contributions. The following shared equation-of-state lesson makes this missing information explicit.

**Read and derive:** [University Physics Volume 2 — OpenStax](../electromagnetism/sources/University-Physics-Volume-2.lingua#r_5JQ0CPQ0RNJNVH175Y9V305J5Q), sections 2.1–2.3, emphasizing the collision assumptions and monatomic limits before later distributions/equipartition material. Recover the wall impulse calculation and the mean-motion decomposition independently. Change particle mass, speed scale and box size and predict the scaling. Explain why the finite fixture cannot validate a real gas or prove equilibrium merely by passing its numerical checks.

